 sync
      " (source) import-path: sync
      " (description) Package sync provides basic synchronization primitives such as mutual exclusion locks. Other than the Once and WaitGroup types, most are intended for use by low-level library routines. Higher-level synchronization is better done via channels and communication.
@sep1 Functions in sync
      OnceFunc
           " (def) "func OnceFunc(f func()) func()"
           " (description) OnceFunc returns a function that invokes f only once. The returned function may be called concurrently.
      OnceValue
           " (def) "func OnceValue[T any](f func() T) func() T"
           " (description) OnceValue returns a function that invokes f only once and returns the value returned by f. The returned function may be called concurrently.
      OnceValues
           " (def) "func OnceValues[T1, T2 any](f func() (T1, T2)) func() (T1, T2)"
           " (description) OnceValues returns a function that invokes f only once and returns the values returned by f. The returned function may be called concurrently.
@sep2 Types in sync
      Cond
           " (description) struct
           " (description) Cond implements a condition variable, a rendezvous point for goroutines waiting for or announcing the occurrence of an event.
      Locker
           " (description) interface
           " (description) A Locker represents an object that can be locked and unlocked.
      Map
           " (description) struct
      Mutex
           " (description) struct
           " (description) A Mutex is a mutual exclusion lock. The zero value for a Mutex is an unlocked mutex.
      Once
           " (description) struct
           " (description) Once is an object that will perform exactly one action.
      Pool
           " (description) struct
           " (description) A Pool is a set of temporary objects that may be individually saved and retrieved.
      RWMutex
           " (description) struct
      WaitGroup
           " (description) struct
           " (description) A WaitGroup is a counting semaphore typically used to wait for a group of goroutines or tasks to finish.

